[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Jetpack Navigation & Deep Linking in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Deep linking allows users to jump directly to a specific destination within your Android application from external sources such as web links, notifications, or other apps. The Jetpack Navigation component simplifies deep link implementation by automatically handling intent extras and back-stack generation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Define Deep Links in the Navigation Graph"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enable a destination to handle deep links, you need to add a <deepLink> element to your navigation graph XML file. This element specifies the URI pattern that the destination should respond to."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<fragment
    android:id="@+id/detailFragment"
    android:name="com.example.app.DetailFragment"
    android:label="Detail"
    tools:layout="@layout/fragment_detail">
    <argument
        android:name="itemId"
        app:argType="string" />
    <deepLink
        app:uri="https://www.example.com/item/{itemId}" />
</fragment>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Configure the NavHost in the Manifest"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, ensure your activity hosting the Navigation Graph is configured in the AndroidManifest.xml. You must include the nav-graph reference so the system knows which navigation XML handles the incoming intents."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<activity
    android:name=".MainActivity"
    android:exported="true">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
    </intent-filter>

    <!-- Deep Link Intent Filter -->
    <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="https" android:host="www.example.com" />
    </intent-filter>

    <meta-data
        android:name="android.app.lib_name"
        android:value="" />
</activity>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Handling Deep Link Arguments in Java"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When the user opens the deep link, the Navigation component automatically extracts the arguments (like itemId) and passes them to the destination fragment's Bundle. You can retrieve them using the generated Safe Args classes."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public class DetailFragment extends Fragment {

    @Override
    public void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState);

        if (getArguments() != null) {
            DetailFragmentArgs args = DetailFragmentArgs.fromBundle(getArguments());
            String itemId = args.getItemId();

            // Use the itemId to fetch and display data
            TextView textView = view.findViewById(R.id.itemTextView);
            textView.setText("Item ID: " + itemId);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Testing Your Deep Links"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can easily test your deep links via the Android Debug Bridge (ADB) command line to verify that the correct fragment loads with the expected arguments."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"adb shell am start -W -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d "https://www.example.com/item/12345" com.example.app"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always set android:autoVerify="true" in your manifest intent filter to enable App Links and bypass the disambiguation dialog for verified domains."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Safe Args to ensure type-safety when extracting deep link arguments inside your Java Fragments."}]
